.\" raz(7) - the raz language at a glance
.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt RAZ 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm raz
.Nd the raz programming language at a glance
.Sh DESCRIPTION
raz is a small systems language for x86-64 Linux, with no garbage
collector and no hidden run time.
This page is what a programmer looks up; the report,
.Pa doc/raz.md
in raz's sources, is the definition, and the numbers in brackets,
such as [6.8], are its sections.
.Xr raz-tutorial 7
is a tour,
.Xr raz-lib 3
the library and
.Xr raz 1
the compiler.
.Sh WORDS AND SYMBOLS
[2] A program is UTF-8; everything outside strings, characters and
comments is ASCII.
.Bl -tag -width "Identifiers"
.It Identifiers
A letter or
.Cm _ ,
then letters, digits and
.Cm _ ,
up to 63 characters; case matters.
.Cm _
alone is the blank.
.It Numbers
.Cm 123 ;
.Cm 0FFH ,
hexadecimal, in capitals;
.Cm 1.5
and
.Cm 2.0E-3 ,
reals, with a digit on both sides of the point.
A number is untyped until it meets a type.
.It Characters
.Cm 'a' ,
the code point, an integer.
.It Strings
.Cm \(dqhello\en\(dq ,
its UTF-8 bytes, ending on its line.
.It Raw strings
.Cm \(gaC:\etmp\(ga ,
the text between backquotes as it stands, no escapes, over lines; if
the opening backquote ends its line and the closing one has only
blanks before it, those blanks come off every line.
.It Escapes
.Cm \en \et \er \e0 \e\e \e' \e\(dq \eu{E9} ,
and in strings
.Cm \exHH ,
any byte.
.It Comments
.Cm "(* ... *)" ,
nesting.
The comment just before a declaration, or before
.Cm module ,
is its documentation.
.El
.Pp
The reserved words, all lowercase but
.Cm VOLATILE :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
and begin break by code const defer div do else elsif end
extern for forward if import in link match mod module not of
or packed proc record repeat return set shl shr then to try
type until var variant VOLATILE while xor
.Ed
.Pp
The basic types,
.Cm ERROR , true , false , nil
and the built-in procedures are predeclared, in a scope around every
module, and may be declared again.
.Sh THE GRAMMAR IN BRIEF
[appendix B]
.Cm "{ x }"
is any number of
.Cm x ,
.Cm "[ x ]"
is one or none.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Module   = "module" Name [ "(" Params ")" ] ";" { Import }
           { "link" ( String | "static" ) ";" }
           [ "check" "off" ";" ]
           { Decl } [ "begin" Stmts ] "end" Name "." .
Import   = "import" Item { "," Item } ";" .
Item     = Name [ ":=" Name [ "(" Type { "," Type } ")" ] ] .
Decl     = "const" { Name ["*"] [ ":" Type ] "=" Expr ";" }
         | "type" { Name ["*"] "=" Type ";" }
         | "var" { Names ":" Type ";" }
         | "proc" Name ["*"] Signature ";" Body ";"
         | "extern" ( "proc" Name ["*"] Signature
                    | "var" Name ["*"] ":" Type ) ";" .
Body     = "forward" | { Local } "begin" Stmts "end" [ Name ]
         | "code" { [ Label ":" ] [ Instr ] ";" } "end" [ Name ] .
Signature = [ "(" Params ")" ] [ ":" ( Type | "(" Types ")" ) ] .
Type     = Name | Module "." Name | "[" Expr "]" Type | "[" "]" Type
         | "^" [ "VOLATILE" ] Type
         | [ "packed" ] "record" Fields "end"
         | "variant" { Case ";" } "end" | "(" Names ")"
         | "set" "of" Type | "proc" Signature .
Stmt     = Designator [ ":=" Expr ] | "_" ":=" Expr
         | Target "," Target { "," Target } ":=" Expr
         | "if" Expr "then" Stmts { "elsif" Expr "then" Stmts }
           [ "else" Stmts ] "end"
         | "while" Expr "do" Stmts "end"
         | "repeat" Stmts "until" Expr
         | "for" Name ":=" Expr "to" Expr [ "by" Expr ]
           "do" Stmts "end"
         | "match" Expr "of" { "|" Arm } "end"
         | "return" [ Exprs ] | "break" | "defer" Stmt .
Arm      = ( Case [ "(" Names ")" ] | Label { "," Label } | "else" )
           ":" Stmts .
Stmts    = { Stmt ";" } .
.Ed
.Pp
Every statement ends in
.Cm \&; ,
even one that ends in
.Cm end
or
.Cm until .
A procedure without parameters leaves out the parentheses where it is
declared and where it is called.
A name is declared before it is used, but for a pointer's type, which
may come later in the same
.Cm type
section, and a
.Cm forward
procedure.
Procedures don't nest.
.Sh MODULES
[3] Module
.Cm M
lives in
.Pa M.raz ,
or
.Pa M.linux-x86_64.raz
for one target's own; it is found beside the main module, then in each
.Fl I
directory, then in the library.
A program has one module of each name, and the library's names are
kept for it.
.Bl -bullet
.It
A name declared with
.Cm *
is exported, and used as
.Cm M.name .
An exported variable is read-only outside its module.
.It
.Cm "import O := Out;"
renames;
.Cm "import Ints := List(INT);"
makes an instance of a generic module, one for each distinct list of
arguments, its procedure arguments named after its types:
.Cm "Map([]U8, INT, Str.hash, Str.eq)" .
.It
A module's body runs once, after those of the modules it imports, and
before the main module's
.Cm main .
.It
.Cm "link \(dqlibX.so.1\(dq;"
loads a C library;
.Cm "link static;"
says the program must not use C.
.Cm "check off;"
leaves the module's index and slice checks out.
.El
.Sh TYPES
[5] Sizes are x86-64's.
.Bl -column "U8 U16 U32 U64" "n * SIZE(T)" "an enumeration, 256 names at most"
.It Sy Type Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Values
.It "I8 I16 I32 I64" Ta "1, 2, 4, 8" Ta "signed, two's complement"
.It "U8 U16 U32 U64" Ta "1, 2, 4, 8" Ta "unsigned"
.It "INT, UINT" Ta "8" Ta "as big as an address"
.It "BOOL" Ta "1" Ta "false, true"
.It "F32, F64" Ta "4, 8" Ta "IEEE 754"
.It "ERROR" Ta "8" Ta "nil, or a constant's error"
.It "^T, proc ..." Ta "8" Ta "an address, or nil"
.It "[]T" Ta "16" Ta "a pointer and a length"
.It "[n]T" Ta "n * SIZE(T)" Ta "n elements, copied whole"
.It "(a, b, c)" Ta "1" Ta "an enumeration, 256 names at most"
.It "set of E" Ta "1, 2, 4, 8" Ta "bit i for E's name i, 64 at most"
.El
.Bl -bullet
.It
A
.Cm record
is laid out as C's struct; a
.Cm "packed record"
has no padding, and its misaligned fields no address.
.It
A
.Cm variant
is an
.Cm INT
tag and the room of its largest case:
.Cm "variant circle(r: F64); dot; end" .
A case is built as
.Cm circle(1.0)
or
.Cm dot ,
and read only by
.Cm match .
.It
.Cm "^VOLATILE T"
points at what something else may change: each access is one access,
of its size, in order [7.6].
.It
.Cm "const NotFound*: ERROR = \(dqnot found\(dq;"
makes an error unlike every other, compared by
.Cm =
and
.Cm # ;
.Cm MSG(e)
is its message.
.It
Every variable starts at zero: 0, false, nil, the empty slice, a
variant's first case.
.It
Named record, variant and enumeration types are each a type of their
own; other types built alike are the same.
.Cm "type A = B;"
is another name for
.Cm B .
.El
.Pp
A value goes where another type is wanted only as an integer constant
that fits it,
.Cm nil
into a pointer, procedure or
.Cm ERROR ,
.Cm ^T
into
.Cm "^VOLATILE T" ,
.Cm [n]T
into
.Cm []T ,
a string into
.Cm []U8 , ^U8
(with a zero byte after it) or
.Cm [n]U8 ,
a procedure into its type, and
.Cm INT
into
.Cm I64 ,
.Cm UINT
into
.Cm U64 ,
wherever it goes, results passed on whole and a
.Cm for Ns 's
bounds too
[5.10].
Anything else is converted by name.
.Sh OPERATORS
[6] From the tightest:
.Bl -column "* / div mod & shl shr" "and, or, not: BOOL; & | xor ~: bits"
.It Sy Operators Ta Sy Notes
.It "not  ~" Ta "not of a BOOL; ~ of an integer's bits"
.It "* / div mod & shl shr" Ta "/ for reals; shifts count modulo the width"
.It "+ - | xor" Ta "a unary minus takes the first term"
.It "= # < <= > >= in" Ta "# is different; in, for a set"
.It "and" Ta "evaluates its right side only if needed"
.It "or" Ta "the same"
.El
.Bl -bullet
.It
Both operands have one type, or one is an untyped constant that fits
the other's, or one is an
.Cm INT
and the other an
.Cm I64
.Pq Cm UINT , U64 ,
and the result is an
.Cm I64 :
with
.Cm "i: I32" ,
.Cm "i < LEN(s)"
is an error.
Integers wrap at their width.
.Cm div
rounds toward zero, and
.Cm mod
takes the dividend's sign; dividing by zero stops the program.
.It
Pointers:
.Cm p^ ,
.Cm "p + i"
and
.Cm "p - i"
in elements,
.Cm "p - q" ,
.Cm p[i] ,
and
.Cm p[lo..hi] ,
a slice; a record's fields through it are
.Cm p^.f .
.It
Arrays and slices:
.Cm a[i] ,
and
.Cm a[lo..hi]
from
.Cm lo
up to but not including
.Cm hi .
Two of integers, BOOLs, enumerations, sets or pointers compare with
.Cm =
and
.Cm # .
.It
Sets:
.Cm +
union,
.Cm *
intersection,
.Cm -
difference,
.Cm <=
and
.Cm >=
subset and superset.
.It
Literals:
.Cm "[1, 2, 3]" ,
an array, and
.Cm "{x: 1, y: 2}" ,
a record with its other fields zero, where the type is known;
.Cm "{red, blue}" ,
a set.
.Cm "const Weekend: []Day = [sat, sun];"
takes its length from its literal [4].
.El
.Sh CONVERSIONS
[6.7] A type's name converts:
.Cm I32(x) , F64(n) , ^Cell(adr) , "^VOLATILE U32(0FEE00000H)" .
.Bl -column "pointer, procedure" "pointer, procedure" "rounded toward zero"
.It Sy From Ta Sy To Ta Sy Result
.It "integer" Ta "integer" Ta "cut, or extended"
.It "integer" Ta "F32, F64" Ta "the nearest"
.It "F32, F64" Ta "integer" Ta "rounded toward zero"
.It "F32 / F64" Ta "F64 / F32" Ta "the same, or the nearest"
.It "enumeration, set" Ta "integer" Ta "its position, its bits"
.It "integer" Ta "enumeration, set" Ta "checked; its low bits"
.It "INT, UINT" Ta "pointer, procedure" Ta "the address"
.It "pointer, procedure" Ta "INT, UINT, pointer" Ta "the address"
.El
.Pp
There is no other: none between
.Cm BOOL
and integers, and none to records, arrays, slices, variants or
.Cm ERROR .
.Sh VIEWS
[6.9]
.Cm v.(T)
is the
.Cm T
whose bytes start where the variable
.Cm v
does: a variable, not a copy, read and written.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mem[16].(I64) := -1;        (* mem[16] to mem[23] *)
k := buf[0].(Header).count;
bits := x.(U64);            (* an F64's bits; I64(x) is its number *)
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm T
is an integer, a real or a pointer, or a record or array of them.
.Cm "a[i].(T)"
lies from
.Cm a[i]
to the end of
.Cm a ,
which is checked as an index is; any other
.Cm v.(T)
lies within
.Cm v .
A view may be misaligned, so it has no address, and a volatile
variable can't be viewed.
.Sh BUILT-IN PROCEDURES
[6.8]
.Bl -tag -width "SYSCALL(n, x1, ...)"
.It Fn SIZE T
The size of type
.Ar T ,
an untyped constant.
.It Fn ADR x
The address of the variable
.Ar x .
.It Fn LEN a
The length of an array, a string constant or a slice.
.It Fn NEW p a
.Ar p
set to a fresh, zeroed
.Ar T
from the allocator
.Ar a ,
a
.Cm ^Mem.Allocator ,
or to nil.
.It Fn MSG e
The message of an
.Cm ERROR .
.It Fn COPY d s
The elements of
.Ar s
copied to the start of
.Ar d ,
as one block, even if they overlap; past
.Fn LEN d ,
a failed check.
.It Fn INC v k , Fn DEC v k
.Ar v
plus or minus
.Ar k ,
or 1; it wraps.
.It Fn CAS p old new
If
.Ar p Ns ^
is
.Ar old ,
set it to
.Ar new
and return true; atomically.
.It Fn XCHG p x , Fn XADD p x
Set, or add to,
.Ar p Ns ^ ,
returning the value before; atomically.
.It Cm FENCE
A full memory barrier.
.It Fn SYSCALL n x1 ...
Linux system call
.Ar n ,
with up to six arguments; minus an errno on failure.
.It Fn ARGC , Fn ARGV
The command line: its count, and its C strings.
.El
.Sh STATEMENTS
[7]
.Bl -tag -width "repeat ... until"
.It Cm "x := e"
Copies; arrays, records and slices whole.
.Cm "a, b := f(x)"
takes several results apart, and
.Cm "_ := f(x)"
drops one on purpose.
.It Cm "f(x)"
A call; a result it drops is warned of.
.It Cm if , while , repeat
On a
.Cm BOOL .
.Cm repeat
runs its body once at least.
.It Cm for
.Cm "for i := a to b by s do ... end" :
.Cm a
and
.Cm b
evaluated once,
.Cm s
a constant, 1 if left out; never steps past
.Cm b ,
and leaves
.Cm i
at the last value it took.
.It Cm match
On a variant, an arm for each case, naming its fields; on an
enumeration, an arm for each name; on an integer, arms of constants
and ranges,
.Cm "| '0' .. '9':" ,
and an
.Cm "| else:" ;
on a string, arms of string constants,
.Cm "| \(dqadd\(dq, \(dqplus\(dq:" ,
and an
.Cm "| else:" ,
which any
.Cm match
may end with.
.It Cm return
With the results, several separated by commas, or
.Cm "return f(x)" ,
passing on
.Cm f Ns 's;
off the end, a procedure returns zeros.
.It Cm break
Out of the innermost
.Cm while , repeat
or
.Cm for .
There is no
.Cm goto
and no
.Cm continue .
.It Cm defer
Runs its statement when the statements it stands in end, by their end,
a
.Cm return
or a
.Cm break ;
the last first.
.El
.Pp
Operands, arguments and an assignment's designators are evaluated left
to right, the designators first [7.5].
.Sh PROCEDURES
[8]
.Bl -bullet
.It
A parameter is a copy; a
.Cm var
one is the caller's variable, of the parameter's type exactly.
.It
.Cm "proc f(x: INT): (INT, ERROR);"
returns several results.
Returning a slice of a local array, of a value parameter, or of an
array literal is an error: the frame it points into is gone [7.3].
An array a call returns lives to the end of its statement [8].
A procedure is a value of its type,
.Cm "proc(a, b: INT): BOOL" ,
or nil.
.It
A
.Cm code
procedure is machine code, in Intel's syntax, called by the System V
convention:
.Cm "rdtsc; shl rdx, 32; or rax, rdx; ret;" .
.It
.Cm "extern proc printf(f: ^U8; ...): I32;"
is C's [9]: its parameters integers, reals, pointers and procedures.
.Cm "extern var environ: ^^U8;"
is C's variable.
A raz procedure of those types is a C callback.
.It
.Cm "proc main*;"
or
.Cm "proc main*: INT;"
of the main module runs after every body; its result is the exit
status.
.Cm Rt.exit
ends the program after the procedures given to
.Cm Rt.atExit
[10].
.El
.Sh WHAT IS CHECKED
[6.5, 10] Each of these stops the program with status 2, or its
signal's, saying on standard error where, why, and the calls under way
with their variables:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
an index past the length, and a slice out of
.Cm "0 <= lo <= hi <= length" ,
unless the module has checks off;
.It
a view that runs past the end of what it is made of, with the same
proviso;
.It
a slice of a pointer that runs backwards, and a number that is no
position of the enumeration it converts to;
.It
a
.Cm nil
pointer, a
.Cm nil
procedure, and a stack overflow;
.It
a division by zero, and the smallest value of a signed type divided by
-1.
.El
.Pp
Integers wrap and reals never stop the program.
The compiler warns of what is declared and not used, of a variable
only assigned, and of a dropped result;
.Fl Werror
makes them errors.
What isn't checked is memory reached around the checks: a stale or
made-up pointer, indexing through a pointer, C, and code procedures.
.Sh LIMITS
This compiler's, beyond the report's 63 characters to a name, 256
names to an enumeration and 64 to a set:
.Bl -column "deferred statements in a procedure" "integers and reals."
.It "a string literal" Ta "65,535 bytes"
.It "a number, as written" Ta "255 characters"
.It "elements of a literal" Ta "1,024"
.It "names in one declaration" Ta "64"
.It "variables in one assignment" Ta "16"
.It "deferred statements in a procedure" Ta "64"
.It "labels in one match" Ta "64"
.It "arguments of a call" Ta "63"
.It "type arguments of a generic module" Ta "8"
.It "scopes nested" Ta "64"
.It "parameters of a code procedure" Ta "6 integers, 8 reals"
.It "procedures given to Rt.atExit" Ta "32"
.It "-I directories" Ta "16"
.It "errors told in one run" Ta "20"
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
